{
  // Static-asset Worker for the built Jekyll site. Deployed by
  // .github/workflows/site-worker.yml on every push to main that touches
  // `site/**` or this file — the same push-to-deploy shape as the API
  // Worker's `worker.yml`, using the same two repo secrets
  // (CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN, CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID). No new secrets needed.
  //
  // This is what algosize.com's non-/api/* traffic is served from — see
  // DEPLOY.md §9 "Move algosize.com off GitHub Pages" for the full cutover
  // (Workers Route + DNS + retiring the GitHub Pages deploy).
  //
  // It used to ALSO be deployed by Cloudflare's own "Workers Builds" git
  // integration — past tense for what deploys THIS config; that trigger is
  // still connected as of this writing and still fires on every push. It
  // turned out to be bound to the WRONG service: it uploads this site's
  // build as a version on `algosize`, the API Worker's service, not on
  // `algosize-site`. Nothing has been routed from it — `versions upload`
  // never routes traffic — but a version that is a static site sitting on
  // the API Worker's service is one accidental promotion away from
  // replacing the live API, and pushes to this repo keep adding more of
  // them. It needs deleting in the Cloudflare dashboard (Workers & Pages →
  // algosize → Settings → Build → disconnect the Git integration) — that
  // is a manual step nothing in this repo can do, and it has NOT been done
  // yet. Until it is, do not promote any version on the `algosize` service
  // without first confirming from its content that it is the API, not this
  // site — `wrangler versions list --name algosize` shows them all.
  //
  // The GitHub Actions workflow this file's header describes is a second,
  // correct deploy path added alongside the still-live bad one, not a
  // replacement for it yet. It runs `wrangler deploy` (not `versions
  // upload`) against exactly this config, from a job that only ever
  // touches the service this file names — so once the dashboard trigger is
  // gone, this is what keeps `algosize-site` deploying.
  //
  // The service name MUST NOT be `algosize`. That is the API Worker's
  // service (see worker/wrangler.toml), deployed by `worker.yml`. With both
  // configs named `algosize`, the two pipelines would deploy different
  // Workers to the same service and the last one to run would win — so an
  // ordinary site change could silently replace the live API, and an API
  // deploy could replace the site. This is not a hypothetical: it is what
  // the still-connected Workers Builds trigger is doing right now.
  //
  // Related: wrangler resolves its config by walking UP the directory tree,
  // so this file also shadows worker/wrangler.toml for any wrangler command
  // run from inside `worker/`. Every wrangler invocation in the repo
  // therefore passes `--config wrangler.toml` explicitly. See DEPLOY.md §2.0.
  //
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The `build` block
  //
  // wrangler's own custom-build hook. It runs BEFORE wrangler reads
  // `assets.directory`, so the directory exists by the time it is needed —
  // `_site_build` is a gitignored artifact, not a committed one, and nothing
  // else in CI produces it.
  //
  // It runs even though this Worker has no `main`. An assets-only config
  // resolves its entry point to wrangler's bundled templates/no-op-worker.js
  // (see resolveEntryWithAssets in the wrangler source), and runCustomBuild
  // is called immediately after that resolution — the entry-point assertion
  // then passes trivially because the no-op template always exists.
  //
  // The command mirrors .github/workflows/jekyll.yml exactly — same config
  // pair, same JEKYLL_ENV — so what deploys here is identical to what the
  // (now-retired) GitHub Pages build produced. `cwd` is relative to the repo
  // root, which is where `wrangler deploy` is invoked from.
  //
  // The Ruby version this runs under is whatever site-worker.yml pins via
  // ruby/setup-ruby — deliberately NOT auto-detected the way Cloudflare's
  // build image did. Ruby 3.4 quietly moved `csv` and `base64` out of
  // default gems into bundled ones, and this exact build broke on it with
  // "cannot load such file -- csv" before site/Gemfile declared them
  // explicitly. Pinning the Ruby version in CI is what stops that class of
  // bug from recurring on some future Ruby release we haven't tested against.
  "$schema": "worker/node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "algosize-site",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-05-11",
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  "build": {
    "cwd": "site",
    "command": "bundle install && JEKYLL_ENV=production bundle exec jekyll build --config _config.yml,_config.production.yml --destination ../_site_build"
  },
  "assets": {
    "directory": "_site_build",
    // Jekyll's default permalinks are directory-style — a post at
    // /blog/foo/ builds to blog/foo/index.html on disk. "auto-trailing-slash"
    // is what makes a request for /blog/foo (no slash) or /blog/foo/ both
    // resolve to that file, matching how GitHub Pages served the same build.
    "html_handling": "auto-trailing-slash",
    // site/404.html renders the real page for anything unmatched, instead of
    // an empty 404 response with no body.
    "not_found_handling": "404-page"
  },
  "compatibility_flags": [
    "nodejs_compat"
  ]
}
